&lt;li&gt;When winter hits, this becomes your priority: be the first back on site in extreme or harsh conditions to assess the lot and get it safe and operational for the team.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Execute snow and ice removal — plowing, shoveling, salting, and brining entry paths, employee lots, load/unload zones, gates, and the tight rows between parked vehicles where plows can&#39;t reach.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run salt-bin execution at Inspection Center doors, and pre-treat and re-treat danger zones — including day-2 refreeze mornings. Provide extended winter support in the 7–10 days after a storm as melt and refreeze continue, not just during the event itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Operate winter equipment safely — snow plow, and with training, trucks with plow and salt-spreader attachments, skid steers, and loaders — always working in your ice cleats.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Metrics:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unit Loss Rate versus Plan&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Safe &amp;amp; Secure App Execution&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inventory Control/Accuracy and Tracking&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Safety Incident Performance&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Snow and ice removal execution and timeliness; salt, brine, and cleat protocol compliance&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you should know/have&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
